* ============================================================================
* index_maintenance_failure_proof.dts
*
* Proves ITEM E: when index maintenance does not happen, the record write
* still lands AND the engine says so.
*
* Contract under test:
*   replaceFieldStored (src/xbase/dbarea.cpp:281-307) returns TRUE with a
*   NON-EMPTY err when the physical write succeeded but the index was not
*   maintained. cmd_REPLACE then marks the field stale and warns:
*     "record written, but index update failed...; REINDEX/REBUILD needed."
*
* SUBJECT CHANGED 2026-07-31 -- READ THIS BEFORE ASSUMING CNX
*   This proof originally ran against CNX/v32, because CnxBackend::upsert/erase
*   were no-ops that set stale_ and returned normally. XIDX-TXN-02 M1 gave CNX
*   real realtime maintenance, so CNX STOPPED BEING A VALID SUBJECT: the
*   warning correctly no longer fires there, and E_T2 inverted to .F.
*
*   The test itself was not the thing that broke -- its subject moved. So it is
*   repointed rather than repurposed, at the backend that still cannot maintain
*   incrementally: CdxNativeBackend::upsert/erase are STILL no-ops that set
*   stale_ and return normally (cdx_native_backend.cpp:507-519). That is the
*   native CDX-V64 path, which is what a RAM/vdisk x64 table uses when LMDB is
*   not in play -- so this now covers the in-memory lane rather than the v32
*   lane, and every marker means exactly what it meant before.
*
*   The realtime CNX behaviour that replaced the old subject has its own proof:
*   cnx_realtime_index_proof.dts (regression CNXLIVE). The two are deliberately
*   separate -- one asserts maintenance HAPPENS, one asserts that when it
*   CANNOT happen the engine is honest about it. Both are contracts.
*
* WHY NOT LMDB
*   The first attempt tried to force an LMDB MDB_MAP_FULL via BUILDLMDB
*   MAPSIZE. That is unreachable: parse_mapsize_spec has an 8 MiB floor
*   (cmd_buildlmdb.cpp:206), and a fixture small enough to author cannot fill
*   8 MiB. A stub backend needs no forcing at all -- it fails to maintain on
*   EVERY replace. That is the routine case, not an edge case, and until
*   wasStale() was wired into apply_replace_snapshot it was entirely silent:
*   the backend reported success while maintaining nothing.
*
* MARKER DESIGN -- READ BEFORE EDITING
*   The first version of this proof PASSED WHILE PROVING NOTHING. Its SEEKs
*   errored out (no active order), the cursor never moved, and the markers
*   re-read whatever record happened to be current, so they printed .T.
*   regardless of index behavior. A test that reports success while not
*   running is AIF-079 instance 8; this file managed to be one.
*
*   Two rules came out of that, and both are load-bearing:
*     1. Park the cursor on a KNOWN DIFFERENT record before every SEEK, then
*        assert the FIELD VALUE moved. A SEEK that silently no-ops now fails
*        the guard instead of inheriting a passing position.
*     2. Markers must be STRING comparisons. "? <str> + FOUND()" and
*        "? <str> + (RECNO() = 1)" both render EMPTY, which reads as failure.
*        ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "X" is a string compare and renders .T./.F.
*        correctly. Wrapping RECNO() in STR() does NOT rescue it -- the
*        problem is RECNO(), not the comparison type.
*
* Self-contained: builds its own throwaway x64 table entirely in the RAM VFS;
* never touches STUDENTS or any live fixture, and writes nothing to disk.
* Teardown unmounts (which drops every RAM file) and restores the x64 env.
*   Standalone:  DO index_maintenance_failure_proof
*   Regression:  REGRESSION RUN IDXSTALE
*
* Runtime status: PROVEN 2026-07-31 on wsl-lean against CNX. Repointed to
* native CDX-V64 the same day; re-prove before trusting the new subject.
* ============================================================================
SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

* in-memory lane: native CDX-V64 is the backend for a RAM x64 table.
* DO mem mounts the RAM VFS and sets the DBF/INDEXES/LMDB slots; the
* unmount/remount is a deterministic clean slate after an aborted run.
DO mem
VDISK UNMOUNT
DO mem

FORMULA "INDEX-STALE-REPORTING-PROOF-BEGIN"

* --- throwaway x64 table, resident in RAM ------------------------------------
CREATE X64 IDXSTALE (SID N(8), LNAME C(20))

APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 1
REPLACE LNAME WITH "MILLER"
APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 2
REPLACE LNAME WITH "ANDERSON"
APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 3
REPLACE LNAME WITH "ZEBRA"
APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 4
REPLACE LNAME WITH "YOUNG"

* --- native CDX-V64 index on LNAME, built in RAM (RUN8, no LMDB) -------------
CDX CREATE
CDX ADDTAG LNAME
REINDEX CDX
SET ORDER TAG LNAME

* --- residency checkpoint: table + index are RAM files, nothing on disk ------
VDISK STATUS

* --- G0: the ORDER IS LIVE ---------------------------------------------------
* Guards everything below. Park on 4 (YOUNG), seek a key that lives on 1. The
* guard is the FIELD VALUE at the cursor, not RECNO(): if the seek no-ops the
* cursor stays on YOUNG and G0 reads .F., so a broken fixture can never be
* mistaken for a proven contract.
GO 4
SEEK "MILLER"
? "E_G0_seek_works_before_mutation:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "MILLER")

* --- G1: baseline ORDER, before any mutation ---------------------------------
* Under LNAME order the first row must be ANDERSON. This is the measurement
* T2 compares against, so it is taken before anything moves.
GO TOP
? "E_G1_baseline_top_is_ANDERSON:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ANDERSON")

* --- the mutation under test -------------------------------------------------
* Expect BOTH lines on the wired path:
*   REPLACE: Replaced field #2 at rec 1.
*   REPLACE: record written, but index update failed...; REINDEX/REBUILD needed.
* Before the wasStale() wiring, only the first line appeared. Trace should read
* "staleBefore=no leftStale=yes" -- that transition IS the reporting contract.
GO 1
REPLACE LNAME WITH "AAAAA"

* --- E_T1: the RECORD WRITE LANDED -------------------------------------------
* The half that must never regress. An unmaintained index must not be
* reported as a failed write; the record is stored.
GO 1
? "E_T1_record_carries_new_value:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "AAAAA")

* --- E_T2: the index is STALE, and provably so -------------------------------
* THE OBSERVABLE IS THE ORDER, NOT THE KEY LOOKUP.
*
* Corrected 2026-07-31 against a real run. The first version asserted that the
* OLD key still resolved and the NEW key did not. Both were wrong, because a
* native SEEK compares LIVE FIELD VALUES while walking a possibly-stale recno
* ordering. Measured after moving MILLER -> AAAAA on a stale index:
*     SEEK "MILLER"  -> Not found.    (no record holds MILLER any more)
*     SEEK "AAAAA"   -> Found at 1.   (record 1 does, and the walk reached it)
* So key lookup can look healthy on a stale index and proves nothing either
* way.
*
* What the stale index actually costs is ORDER. Record 1 still occupies the
* slot MILLER sorted into (after ANDERSON), so an ordered walk still starts at
* ANDERSON even though AAAAA now sorts first. That is directly observable and
* is what a stale index means to a reader.
*
* If this ever reads .F., the backend under test has GAINED realtime
* maintenance. That is good news, not a regression -- but it means this proof
* needs repointing again, exactly as it was repointed off CNX.
GO TOP
? "E_T2_stale_order_still_starts_at_ANDERSON:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ANDERSON")

* --- E_T3: SEEK still reaches the record (documents the real behavior) -------
* Not a staleness assertion -- a guard against over-claiming. Recorded so the
* next reader does not repeat the wrong model above.
GO 4
SEEK "AAAAA"
? "E_T3_seek_reaches_moved_record_despite_stale_order:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "AAAAA")

* --- E_T4: REINDEX is the documented remedy, and it works --------------------
* The warning tells the operator to REINDEX/REBUILD. Prove that advice is good
* rather than merely well-phrased: after the rebuild the order is correct and
* AAAAA sorts first, which is exactly what T2 showed it was not.
REINDEX CDX
SET ORDER TAG LNAME
GO TOP
? "E_T4_after_rebuild_top_is_AAAAA:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "AAAAA")

FORMULA "INDEX-STALE-REPORTING-PROOF-END"

* --- teardown ----------------------------------------------------------------
* No bare "SET ORDER TO" -- it reports "SET ORDER: missing target."
* CLOSE drops the order with the table; UNMOUNT drops every RAM file, so no
* ERASE is needed and nothing can be left behind on disk.
CLOSE
VDISK UNMOUNT
DO x64
